WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.070
Film Courage: I’m wondering if can talk
about short films for a moment?

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:08.960
And you talk about how you made your first
short film and when you look back at it now

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.019
there are things that you’re really proud
about?

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:16.090
And some can you also touch on some of the
misconceptions filmmakers have about short

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.431
films and I know this is your opinion, it’s
not law but you had some opinions about it

00:00:20.431 --> 00:00:21.431
and we’d love to hear it?

00:00:21.431 --> 00:00:22.431
Chris von Hoffmann, writer/director of DRIFTER,
MONSTER PARTY, WHITE TRASH and more: Yeah,

00:00:22.431 --> 00:00:28.140
I understand that a lot of people are very
anti-short films.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.760
I hear these people saying “No one cares
about short films.”

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.750
And I mean, there’s an Academy Award for
short films.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.270
Obviously some people care about them but
I get what they’re saying that feature films

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.270
(that makes sense).

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.880
I was making shorts for years and none of
them were going anywhere and then boom!

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.460
I make a feature and then it gets a lot of
exposure and all that stuff.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:01:00.620
So I understand that but I wouldn’t have
been able to approach the feature that I did

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.690
if it wasn’t for all those shorts that I
was making.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:09.140
I think short films for me I started to realize
over the years that they’re not at all about

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.570
getting representation through those or getting
a big movie out of those.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.500
There was a time that that happened.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.610
I am shocked when people make one five minute
short that goes viral and they get a twenty

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.500
million dollar movie.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.000
I just don’t really understand that at all.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.740
I think you really need to earn your stripes
a little bit.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:36.690
But I think shorts are much more about sculpting
your aesthetic and learning to tell a story

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:42.659
and working with actors, working with truncated
schedules, and just understanding how movies

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:44.729
are made before you jump into a feature.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:49.590
Making a feature versus making a short, it
really is night and day.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.640
I mean it’s such a completely different
experience.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.570
Like you need to get those warm-up periods
and maybe make shorts that are like 5 minutes,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:02.740
10 minutes, then maybe a 20 minute short,
getting a little closer to stretching out

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.869
a story a little bit more.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:14.420
Everyone has their theories on things and
I don’t want to knock anything down but

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.680
when people say “I don’t care about shorts,
I just graduated film school.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.950
I just want to make a feature right now.”

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.090
I hear that a lot from people.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:31.840
Or people just want to make one short and
then jump into a feature and I remember Rian

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.541
Johnson said something (of the newest STAR
WARS movie) and he was saying “Make like

00:02:36.541 --> 00:02:40.150
30 short films if you can.”

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.530
Make as many mistakes as possible because
that’s probably the biggest thing.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.750
Because making movies is always sort of like
success training.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:54.010
Experiment, mess up a lot, and always go for
the most ambitious projects.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.870
Always try to approach something that is hard.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:02.160
And I think a lot of people get so cautious
about them, I think they are so afraid of

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.160
failure.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:10.989
I think they are so terrified of making a
movie that’s a little too dense or a little

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:19.130
too challenging for them to approach in a
short schedule or with no money.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:25.710
Because if you make something that is super
ambitious and maybe you do totally fail, but

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:32.410
I would rather make something that’s ambitious
and it challenged me so much and it trained

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:33.879
me for the next movies.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:40.450
And perhaps maybe fail at it than make something
that’s just two people talking to each other

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:46.930
and is just some easy movie just because it’s
more economically realistic.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:51.940
But I don’t think that’s going to train
you.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:58.480
I think they can train you on somewhat of
a level but I think if you can do something

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.849
really ambitious in a very tight budget like
someone like Robert Rodriguez or John Carpenter

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:05.850
or Edgar Wright even.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:10.959
I mean those are guys that I look up to.

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:15.060
I understand that Robert Rodriguez and John
Carpenter score their own music and do a lot

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.499
of their own stuff but their mentality about
always going for the more ambitious project

00:04:19.499 --> 00:04:25.389
and just creating a bunch because John Carpenter
made so many short films like DARK STAR and

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:29.699
then Rodriguez made so many short films before
he made EL MARIACHI and I just think that

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:35.810
short films are very important to do because
I think if you completely neglect them, I

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:45.879
almost sort of think it’s like somewhat
disrespectful I think to moviemaking because

00:04:45.879 --> 00:04:50.979
what makes you think you’re so good to jump
into a feature right away?

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:55.620
Scorsese made so many shorts even before Who’s
That Knocking At My Door.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:01.669
So I’m a very much pro short films and make
as many as possible and just really screw

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:04.860
up as much as possible, make as many mistakes
as possible.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.009
Because one day you might make a movie that
where everything just clicks but you’re

00:05:09.009 --> 00:05:13.159
not going to be able to make that movie until
you make those previous movies that you’ve

00:05:13.159 --> 00:05:21.240
learned from all those mistakes and just don’t
overthink things, just keep creating.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.090
Keep making stuff.

